A||||6||fiero56||Victory!!!||||6
Z||000000||fiero56||11-25-1999||02:50 PM||||Man does that feel good! I finally got the transmission off about 5 minutes ago.  There was a dust shield in the way of the flywheel that held me up for a good 45 minutes. Now that it's all opened up, all I have to do is pull the old stuff off(pressure plate, clutch disk, throwout bearing) and put in the new. <BR>One question for you guys though. This is regarding the Centerforce clutch debate.  It has been said that the "Centerforce" clutch kits are not actually by Centerforce and that the big reason to stay away from them is the pressure plate with "LUK" stamped on it.  Well guess what, That same "LUK" stamp is on my factory pressure plate.  What gives? Is this the same thing as what Centerforce supplies in their kits?||||reg||6
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||11-26-1999||01:20 AM||||In the past we have seen LUK on preasure plates that have a kind of "swirl" shape on the preasure fingers of it. This is the one that we avoid. However, we have found one preasure plate with LUK on it without the "swirl" design. We have yet to see another one. If your tranny has never been cracked open and its factory AND its NOT the swirl design, I would love to hear more. It is the swirl design with missing fingers (owners have said it sounded like a granade going off.) on it that we have displayed at the shop.<P>The LUK design preasure plate with swirl designed holders is what we have seen come from Centerforce in the past simply painted orange. I hasten to add that this is not a knock on ALL Centerforce clutches! Just the ones for Fieros that we have seen.<P>Hope this is helpful and clarifying. I really like this site and don't want to ruffle feathers.||||reg||10
Z||000002||fiero56||11-26-1999||09:47 AM||||Yeap,<BR>It has "LUK" stamped on it, but it also has the "swirl" design to it.  It has 152K miles on it(everything is original), most are easy miles, just once or twice when I had to show myself what it could do <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. All the fingers are where they belong(both on the plate and my hands) and I never had any problems with it. My new one from RAM doesn't have "LUK" stamped on it, and it doesn't have the swirl design either.  I hope it lasts another 150K.<P>Last question: Does GM still put on these "faulty" pressure plates, or have they changed to another?||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||11-26-1999||11:01 AM||||To be honest I didn't know that the factory was putting LUKs in. I will have to ask around for that. If they are putting it in currently I wouldn't know. We are currently getting quite a number of clients from the dealers. We have some service writers telling people that they have to tear out the whole dashboard to get to the heater! Oh sure! The stories we have been hearing the PONTIAC(!) dealers telling these owners is frightening. It almost like the dealers themselves are the source of all of the misinformation out there!<P>PS Good to hear about the fingers, both mechanical as well your own!<P>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 11-26-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 11-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-28-1999||09:45 PM||||Went on vacation for the week-end so I just pulled the disk and pressure plate this afternoon.  The clutch disk has "LUK" stamped all over it too.  <BR>For 152,000 miles, I'd say this disk has no more than 40% wear on it at the most(probobly less).  I guess when the guy I bought it from said "highway miles" he meant it. I'm pretty sure it is the original clutch though.  All 4 springs are pretty loose, the "hub"(I don't have a better name) is too, and both sides of the friction material are pretty well squished together. It has wear on it, just not "friction" wear. No heat burns, everything is worn evenly, and not a single scratch on the flywheel. I'm impressed, GM put in a product that held up for a while. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||11-28-1999||10:16 PM||||WKDFIRO<BR>When I replaced mine in 1996, the one I pulled out was an LUK and the fingers were straight.  This is the one I paid a Pontiac dealer around 400 bucks just for the parts when they replaced it in 1991.  Disc had 4 springs.<BR>The pressure plate RAM sent me was identical, but they put in a 6 spring disc.  P/N 88644, $140 from Summit for the kit.<BR>Any idea what the differences in these plates would be?  I have replaced clutches at roughly 60,000 and 125,000 miles, when they started to slip noticeably.  I thrash on it pretty hard.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-29-1999||10:08 PM||||the springs that are around the center of the disk provide rotational impact absorbtion for the disk. Stiffer and/or more springs take a bigger pounding. <P>The wave spring between the friction material layers provides slower engagement. Sometime you can see this, sometimes not. Sometimes it's not there. depends on who made it, and for what application.<P>The spring package in the disk helps copntrol clutch engage rate. The engage rate determines slip when you slam the clutch. You must have some slip or you break the clutch, but to much and you cook it. It's a very tricky thing to work out.<P>I've even seen bigger clutch disks with spring in spring sets in the center like some heavy duty valve spring sets have.||||reg||1
